---
<BaseLayout title="Pre-treatment estimates — Dana" description="Making insurance estimates easier to find, submit, and track for Canada Life plan members.">
  <header class="case-hero"><div class="container"><a class="back" href="/#work">← Selected work</a><p class="eyebrow">Canada Life · Released product</p><h1>Making pre-treatment estimates easier to find and track</h1><p class="lead">A research-led redesign that made a buried, time-sensitive service easy to find, submit, and track.</p></div></header>

  <section class="tldr" aria-label="Project summary"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">At a glance</p><ul class="tldr-list"><li><b>Problem</b><span>Members couldn’t find pre-treatment estimates because the option was buried inside claim categories.</span></li><li><b>What I did</b><span>As lead UX designer, I combined a 25-person tree test, 24,177 recorded submissions, and 1,476 survey comments to design a dedicated estimates path with two entry points.</span></li><li><b>Result</b><span>The primary journey went from seven steps to four, and the redesign shipped in the Canada Life group-benefits portal.</span></li></ul></div></section>

  <section class="snapshot"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Project snapshot</p><h2>A dedicated path for estimates</h2><p>An estimate, also called a predetermination, lets members check expected coverage before treatment. I led the UX for a standalone path to submit and track estimates in the group-benefits portal, designed within Salesforce Industries and Lightning Web Component constraints.</p></div><dl><div><dt>My role</dt><dd>Lead UX Designer</dd></div><div><dt>Users</dt><dd>Group-benefits members planning healthcare expenses</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Research synthesis, information architecture, flows, UI, handoff, and design QA</dd></div><div><dt>Partners</dt><dd>Product, research, content, business analysis, engineering, accessibility, and QA</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Released in the Canada Life group-benefits portal</dd></div></dl></div></section>

  <section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">The problem</p><h2>Members could not find the service when they needed it</h2><p class="large">Before an important procedure, members needed to know what their plan might cover. But the estimate option sat inside other claim categories, the terminology was inconsistent, and there was no obvious place to track a submitted request.</p><div class="challenge"><strong>Design challenge</strong><p>How might we make estimates and predeterminations easy to find, submit, and track—without requiring members to understand the organization’s internal terminology?</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Evidence</p><h2>Three sources revealed a findability problem</h2><p>I combined behavioural data, information-architecture research, and unstructured customer feedback.</p></div><div class="evidence-grid"><article><strong>25</strong><h3>Tree-test participants</h3><p>For estimate submission, 10 chose Make a claim and 9 chose Benefits. For tracking, 16 chose Benefits and 6 chose Overview—evidence that one hidden path would not match everyone’s mental model.</p></article><article><strong>24,177</strong><h3>Recorded submissions</h3><p>Adobe Analytics recorded 20,032 English and 4,145 French estimate submissions over a 13-month period, demonstrating meaningful demand for the service.</p></article><article><strong>1,476</strong><h3>Qualtrics comments reviewed</h3><p>Recurring themes included difficulty finding estimates, unclear terminology, and uncertainty about where to track a submitted request.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="flows"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Information architecture</p><h2>From a concealed route to deliberate entry points</h2><p>The flow redesign separated estimates from unrelated claim categories and added access from locations supported by the tree-test findings.</p></div><div class="flow-compare"><figure><a href="/images/estimates/old-flow.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/estimates/old-flow.png" alt="Old estimate submission flow showing a long path through claim categories" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>Before · Embedded path</b><span>Estimates were not a primary destination. Members found them by remembering where to look, exploring the claim flow, or asking an insurance representative.</span></figcaption></figure><figure><a href="/images/estimates/new-flow.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/estimates/new-flow.png" alt="New estimate submission flow with dedicated entry points and a shorter route" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>After · Recognizable, direct path</b><span>Estimates became a visible destination with a short route, so members know where to begin without prior knowledge or help.</span></figcaption></figure></div>

    <div class="walkthrough-head"><p class="eyebrow">Interactive comparison</p><h3>Walk through both experiences</h3><p>Compare how the entry point, terminology, and route changed.</p></div>
    <div class="prototype-compare">
      <article><div class="prototype-label"><div><span>Before</span><h3>Previous estimate flow</h3></div><a href={oldPrototype} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Figma ↗</a></div><div class="figma-frame"><iframe src={oldPrototypeEmbed} title="Interactive walkthrough of the previous Canada Life estimate flow" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><p>A concealed route that depended on familiarity or assistance.</p></article>
      <article><div class="prototype-label"><div><span>After</span><h3>Redesigned estimate flow</h3></div><a href={newPrototype} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Figma ↗</a></div><div class="figma-frame"><iframe src={newPrototypeEmbed} title="Interactive walkthrough of the redesigned Canada Life estimate flow" loading="lazy" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><p>A visible destination with a direct path to the task.</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="embed-note">If a prototype does not load in the page, use “Open in Figma” to launch the walkthrough in a separate tab.</p>
  </div></section>

  <section><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Design exploration</p><h2>Testing how the estimate entry point should communicate</h2><p>I explored combinations of content, controls, and component patterns, then aligned with the team on the direction that best balanced clarity with Salesforce component constraints.</p></div><figure class="wide-artifact"><a href="/images/estimates/component-iterations.png" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><img src="/images/estimates/component-iterations.png" alt="A collection of estimate start-page component and content variations" loading="lazy"/></a><figcaption><b>Component and content iterations</b><span>Alternatives varied hierarchy, selection patterns, and how much guidance appears before a member starts.</span></figcaption></figure></div></section>

  <section class="tinted"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Design decisions</p><h2>Four changes made the service easier to understand</h2><div class="decisions"><article><span>01</span><h3>Use both “estimate” and “predetermination”</h3><p>Customer language and insurance terminology appear together, reducing the burden of knowing the official term.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Create more than one credible entry point</h3><p>Members can enter from Make a claim and the left navigation, reflecting the different locations revealed through tree testing.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Give estimates a dedicated landing page</h3><p>Required documents, starting the request, and tracking prior submissions are gathered into one recognizable destination.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Reduce the primary journey</h3><p>The redesigned structure reduced the documented flow from seven steps to four while preserving necessary insurance information.</p></article></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container grid responsive"><div><p class="eyebrow">Responsive experience</p><h2>One hierarchy across desktop and mobile</h2><p>Understanding requirements, starting an estimate, and finding submitted estimates stay prominent on every screen size. I reviewed these decisions with engineering throughout delivery.</p></div><div class="mockups"><img src="/images/estimates/desktop-entry.png" alt="Desktop mockup of the released estimate entry experience" loading="lazy"/><img src="/images/estimates/mobile-entry.png" alt="Mobile mockup of the estimate entry experience" loading="lazy"/></div></div></section>

  <section class="prototype"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Experience in motion</p><h2>Web and mobile walkthroughs</h2><p>These recordings show the final flow across both responsive contexts.</p></div><div class="videos"><article><div class="video-frame"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/L02cehfR18A" title="Estimate experience, web view" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><h3>Web view</h3></article><article><div class="video-frame mobile-video"><iframe src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/KZ-hdAeZ4_U" title="Estimate experience, mobile view" loading="lazy" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><h3>Mobile view</h3></article></div></div></section>

  <section class="impact"><div class="container"><div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Outcome</p><h2>A dedicated, shorter estimate journey</h2></div><div class="impact-grid impact-grid--two"><article><strong>7 → 4</strong><p>steps in the primary journey, with required insurance information preserved</p></article><article><strong>Released</strong><p>responsive experience live in the Canada Life group-benefits portal</p></article></div><p class="caveat">Post-launch call-volume and satisfaction data aren’t included here. To measure impact, I would track monthly estimate submissions and support contacts asking where to find estimates.</p></div></section>

  <section class="dark"><div class="container grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Reflection</p><h2>Findability is part of the product—not a layer around it</h2></div><div><p>A well-designed form cannot help members who never find it, so navigation, language, and tracking have to be designed as part of the service.</p><p>Combining several evidence sources also kept me from treating one entry point as obvious. Members hold different mental models of the same task, so the final structure offers more than one way in.</p></div></div></section>

  <section><div class="container narrow"><p class="eyebrow">Next project</p><h2>Dialogue: account-management portal</h2><p class="large">A discovery project on separating internal operations from customer self-service.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/work/dialogue-account-management/">Read the next case study</a><a class="button button--secondary" href="/#work">Back to selected work</a></div></div></section>

